he exit code and continuing to the next directory, then after processing all entries I'm exiting with the appropriate code. Now I'm moving into the flag parsing logic, which handles command-line arguments including support for combined short flags and the `--` terminator.\n\nParsing flag options... Handling the filter logic for directory entries — when the all-files flag is set, I'm prepending the current and parent directories, then iterating through the actual entries and skipping hidden files unless the filter allows them.\n\nNow I'm defining the makeEntry function that constructs a file entry, optionally calling lstat to gather metadata if needed for sorting. Then I'm setting up the sortEntries function to order entries based on the configured sort mode...\n\nImplementing sort logic... Still writing comparison logic... Writing utility functions...\n\nI'm realizing the sorting logic needs careful handling—file operands and directory operands both get sorted as fileEntry objects using the same sortEntries function, which respects the active sort mode. For entries within directories, I create them with makeEntry, which only stats them when necessary (for time or size sorting, but not for name sorting). I don't actually need that reverseStrings helper since sortEntries already handles reverse sorting. Now I'm thinking through edge cases to make sure the implementation is solid. I need to figure out whether the header should appear based on the total number of command-line arguments or just the successful ones. Looking at how GNU ls behaves, it shows the header whenever there are multiple arguments passed, regardless of whether some fail—so `ls nonexistent dir1` would still display \"dir1:\" because two arguments were given. I should use `len(operands) > 1` to check the original argument count rather than counting only the valid files and directories. I need to fix the `multipleOperands` logic to check the original operand count instead of the count of successfully p